---
import GuideLayout from "@layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const title = "State Total-Loss Threshold Laws | FairClaimCalculator";
const description =
  "Every U.S. state sets its own total-loss threshold — a fixed percentage or a formula — deciding when an insurer must total your car instead of repairing it.";
const path = "/guides/total-loss/state-total-loss-threshold-laws/";
---
<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  h1="State Total-Loss Threshold Laws Explained"
  cluster={{ name: "Total Loss", href: "/guides/total-loss/" }}
  publishedDate="2026-08-01"
  reviewedDate="2026-08-01"
  summary="Whether your damaged car gets repaired or declared a total loss can depend on which state you're in as much as how bad the damage is. States use one of two different methods, and the exact number varies from 60% to 100% of your car's value."
  sources={[
    'Brennan, R., "Total Loss Threshold by State," Policygenius, updated July 2026 — the primary source for the state-by-state table below. Policygenius publishes its editorial standards and reviews this table periodically; state laws and administrative rules can change between reviews, so treat the table as a starting point, not a final legal citation, and confirm your own state\'s current rule with your state insurance department.',
    'New Hampshire Insurance Department, <a href="https://www.insurance.nh.gov/about-us/property-casualty-division/list-accepted-valuation-methods-total-loss" target="_blank" rel="noopener">List of Accepted Valuation Methods for Total Loss</a> — a real example of a state regulator publishing its own accepted methodology, referenced here and in the companion guide below.',
  ]}
  relatedLinks={[
    { label: "Total-Loss Threshold Checker calculator", href: "/calculators/total-loss-threshold-checker/" },
    { label: "How Insurers Value a Total-Loss Vehicle", href: "/guides/total-loss/how-insurers-value-a-total-loss-vehicle/" },
    { label: "How to Dispute a Total-Loss Valuation", href: "/guides/total-loss/how-to-dispute-a-total-loss-valuation/" },
    { label: "Total-Loss Offer Audit calculator", href: "/calculators/total-loss-offer-audit/" },
  ]}
>
  <h2>Two different methods, one decision</h2>
  <p>
    Every state requires insurers to total a car once repair costs cross a certain point relative to
    its value — but states don't agree on how to measure that point. There are two approaches in use
    across the U.S.:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Simple percentage threshold.</strong> If repair cost reaches a fixed percentage of the
      vehicle's actual cash value (ACV), it's a total loss. Example: a $10,000 car in a 75%-threshold
      state becomes a total loss once repairs reach $7,500 — regardless of salvage value.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Total Loss Formula (TLF).</strong> A car is a total loss once <em>repair cost + expected
      salvage value</em> meets or exceeds ACV. Using the same $10,000 car with $7,500 in damage and an
      estimated $1,000 salvage value: $7,500 + $1,000 = $8,500, which is still under $10,000 — so this
      same damage would <em>not</em> be a total loss under TLF, even though it would be under a
      75%-only rule.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="limits-note">
    <strong>Same damage, different outcome.</strong> This is exactly why the same accident can total a
    car in one state and not in the one next door — it isn't just about how bad the damage looks, it's
    about which formula your state uses and what percentage or salvage figure gets plugged in.
  </div>

  <h2>Total-loss threshold by state</h2>
  <p>
    The table below reflects a July 2026 compiled review by Policygenius, a licensed insurance
    broker that publishes and periodically updates state-by-state insurance comparisons. It is a
    secondary compilation, not a citation to each state's statute directly — always verify your
    state's current rule with your state insurance department before relying on a specific number.
  </p>
